<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="37cf60d3">&lt;p&gt;
    Status reports provide information on work plan activities, issues, risks, change requests, milestones, and
    deliverables.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Status reporting – sample process&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../core.mgmt.common.base/workproducts/./resources/StatusReport.png&quot; width=&quot;512&quot; height=&quot;289&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="32e275e7"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="88e79ebf">Provide information in a report format at an appropriate level for each audience. The format may range from a simple
status report to more elaborate reports and may be prepared regularly or on an exception basis.</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="d968f23c"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="91c283b0">&lt;p&gt;
    Project Status reporting provides information to help projects deliver on time and on budget with the specified
    quality.  Project reporting provides leadership and executive sponsors with the information necessary to
    successfully assist the team and resolve project issues.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Status reporting helps facilitate regular communications between the Business team leads, PMs, the PMO, leadership and
    stakeholders regarding project and activity/work package status, including risks and dependencies.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="4497f075"></attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="901d85e5"></attribute>
<attribute name="briefOutline" nodeId="a5fa02">&lt;p&gt;
    The common elements of Project Status Reports are:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Project health&lt;/strong&gt; - summary of the project’s health status (e.g., cost, schedule, scope, resource);
        this is derived from the individual status reports submitted by the team members to the team lead or pm
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Individual project progress assessments&lt;/strong&gt; - narrative overview of project status including
        accomplishments, future plans and barriers to completion (e.g., the executive summary).
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Issues&lt;/strong&gt; - summary of any issues that have been identified, are open, have been resolved or have
        been rejected
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Risks&lt;/strong&gt; - summary of any risks pertaining to the project(s)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Change requests&lt;/strong&gt; - summary of any change requests that have been identified, have been resolved or
        have been rejected.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Work products&lt;/strong&gt; - summary of any work products that have been created, completed or removed from the
        scope of the project
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Project milestones&lt;/strong&gt; - summary of any progress milestones such as milestones description, milestone
        status, milestone due date, and milestones estimate to complete.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Project metrics&lt;/strong&gt; - summary of any progress metrics such as earned value, spi, cpi and projected
        completion date, and effort specific metrics such as hours per program, lines of code, system down time and fatal
        errors on development projects (as applicable).
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
